  "response": "The most likely cause is temporal inconsistency in `generateInvoice`: `src/billing.ts` reads the wall clock seven times, including five `Date.now()` calls across lines 57–250. Near midnight, two reads in one invocation can fall on different dates, producing flaky boundary behavior.\n\nThe large 204-line function increases the chance those reads affect separate billing rules that implicitly expect the same “now.” The weak billing tests also provide little protection: one has no assertion, and another only has a weak assertion.\n\nProposed fix:\n\n```ts\ntype Clock = {\n  now(): number;\n};\n\nexport function generateInvoice(\n  user: User,\n  clock: Clock = { now: Date.now },\n): Invoice {\n  const now = clock.now(); // Capture exactly once.\n  const currentDate = new Date(now);\n\n  // Pass `now` or `currentDate` to extracted calculation helpers.\n}\n```\n\nIn tests, inject a fixed instant immediately before, at, and after the relevant boundary:\n\n```ts\nconst clock = {\n  now: () => Date.parse(\"2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\"),\n};\n```\n\nKeep all billing calculations in one time domain—preferably UTC—and avoid constructing additional dates from the live clock. If `billing.ts` calls helpers in `src/date.ts`, fix those in the same path: the scan reports mixed UTC/local access, timezone-ambiguous parsing, and DST-naive day arithmetic there.\n\nI would first make the clock injectable and capture it once, then extract the date-sensitive portions of `generateInvoice` into pure helpers and add exact assertions for totals, timestamps, and due dates at UTC/day/month boundaries.",
